"# aos-loop: run a body again and again\n\n```sh\nAOS_STACK=/tmp/st aos-loop --max 3 loop.json    # loop.json: {\"exec\": {\"argv\": [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"echo run\"]}}\nAOS_STACK=/tmp/st aos-loop --interval-ms 100 loop2.json\n```\n\nEach run's exit code: 0 = run again, 10 = stop with exit 0, other = stop with that code (`aos-loop: error: RunFailed run 1: loop3.json#/exec failed with exit code 4`). It needs `AOS_STACK` for its ctl window (`StackMissing`). `on_start` / `on_stop` (written like exec) run once; on_stop gets `AOS_LOOP_CODE`. `--ctl`, `--state`, `--state-flux` choose the windows; `{\"cmd\": \"pause\"}`, resume and stop in the ctl window control it (that is what aos-daemon-ctl pause writes). `--enable-logs` prints one JSON line per run. Agents and daemon rows are loops started by a daemon; you seldom start one yourself.\n"
